The host stays in the courtyard while the second player leaves and returns. Both games stay running; this clip shows a session reconnect. The loop cuts out the save and connection waits. [url=https://christophervr.github.io/kotor-coop/media/showcase/leave-and-rejoin.mp4]Watch or pause this clip.[/url]

[b]About these clips:[/b] staged local demonstrations of selected features. God mode sustained combat filming in the earlier trailer. Normal combat balance, a complete campaign and independent internet play remain unverified. [url=https://christophervr.github.io/kotor-coop/#trailer]Full trailer with sound[/url] | [url=https://github.com/ChristopherVR/kotor-coop/blob/main/site/media/showcase/capture-20261030.json]New capture details[/url] | [url=https://github.com/ChristopherVR/kotor-coop/blob/main/site/media/showcase/manifest.json]Clip sources and edits[/url].

[size=5][b]Install, host, connect[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]Download the Main file[/b] and extract its ZIP to find [b]kotor-coop-<version>-setup.exe[/b].
[*][b]Close KOTOR and run setup.[/b] It finds your GOG or Steam copy, or lets you choose the folder. Allow the firewall rule if you want to host. Use setup, not Vortex: the mod installs beside the game executable.
[*][b]Start KOTOR → Co-op → Host.[/b] Use an existing save, or a new game after the first autosave.
[*][b]Your friend chooses Co-op → Connect[/b] and selects the host or enters the host's address. On the first join, the normal character-creation screen lets them make their own character.
[/list]

[b]Everyone needs the same co-op version.[/b] Start with matching game mods too. Internet hosting may need port forwarding or a tunnel; the mod does not provide a relay server. [url=https://christophervr.github.io/kotor-coop/play.html]Host and join guide.[/url]

While connected, load the save called [b]Co-op[/b] to resume the session. Your own autosave is backed up as [b]Autosave before co-op[/b]. Press [b]Enter[/b] to chat and [b]~[/b] for the console.

Prefer a plain ZIP? [url=https://github.com/ChristopherVR/kotor-coop/releases/latest]GitHub releases[/url] also include the Windows package: extract it into its own folder, not over the game, and run [b]KotorCoop.Agent.exe[/b]. The files are not code-signed, so SmartScreen may warn. Releases include SHA-256 checksums; report antivirus detections rather than adding exclusions.

[size=5][b]What works today[/b][/size]
These are summaries of recorded results. Read the catches before planning a long session.
[list]
[*][b]Joining and reconnecting:[/b] host from the game menu, join as your own character, leave and return. Separate-PC internet and automatic router setup need wider testing.
[*][b]Exploration and travel:[/b] see each other, change areas and take the same planet trip. Brief position correction can happen after loading.
[*][b]Combat:[/b] shared enemies and group scaling have been demonstrated. Full-campaign balance remains unverified.
[*][b]Dialogue and story:[/b] share conversations and quest progress. One speaker chooses replies; recent dialogue fixes still need ordinary retesting.
[*][b]Items and shops:[/b] personal backpacks and credits, shared quest items and merchant stock. The host controls companion equipment. Simultaneous container access needs retesting.
[*][b]Saves and recovery:[/b] co-op saves, loads and rejoining after a game disconnect. Unsaved progress can be lost; a host helper crash requires choosing Host again.
[*][b]Minigames:[/b] Pazaak, swoop racing and turrets are single-seat. One player plays; the others wait.
[*][b]Larger parties:[/b] up to eight can connect, and group travel and combat have been demonstrated. Start with two; larger-group campaign reliability is experimental.
[/list]

[size=5][b]Still being tested[/b][/size]
[b]A complete two-player campaign and full ending chain have not been verified.[/b] Recent fixes for dialogue over equipment menus, Trask disappearing, container access, save-name input, subtitles/skipping and the post-save enemy banner need ordinary in-game confirmation. Independent-PC networking, Steam friend invites, long sessions and larger groups also need wider playtesting.

[url=https://github.com/ChristopherVR/kotor-coop/blob/main/docs/limitations.md]Activity-by-activity limitations[/url]  |  [url=https://github.com/ChristopherVR/kotor-coop/blob/main/docs/RELEASE-READINESS.md]Current release checklist[/url]

[size=5][b]Requirements and compatibility[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]KOTOR 1 v1.0.3, GOG or Steam,[/b] lawfully owned by each player. KOTOR 2 is not supported.
[*][b]64-bit Windows[/b] is the verified gameplay baseline. GitHub also carries a Linux / Steam Deck tarball for Proton or Wine; its installer is tested, but Linux gameplay remains unverified.
[*]Matching co-op versions and backed-up saves. Start with matching game mods; the connection checks explain differences, but do not guarantee compatibility with every mod.
[*]For two instances on one PC, use separate game folders and at least one GOG copy; Steam allows only one running copy.
[*]The baseline captures use the game's 800×600 view. Full widescreen and replacement-menu compatibility remain under test.
[/list]

[size=5][b]Updates, removal and support[/b][/size]
Setup preserves coop.ini settings and can chain alongside another dinput8.dll mod such as ReShade. The game executable stays unchanged. Remove the mod from Windows Installed apps, or run [b]KotorCoop.Agent.exe uninstall[/b]. Your Co-op save and character are kept.

For a bug report, include the version, store, player count, other mods and steps to reproduce. Double-click [b]KotorCoop.CollectLogs.cmd[/b] for a support ZIP; review logs for passwords, addresses and personal paths before sharing. Never attach game files.

[center][url=https://github.com/ChristopherVR/kotor-coop/issues]Report a bug[/url]  |  [url=https://github.com/ChristopherVR/kotor-coop/blob/main/docs/TROUBLESHOOTING.md]Troubleshooting[/url]  |  [url=https://github.com/ChristopherVR/kotor-coop]Source and documentation[/url][/center]

[size=5][b]About the project[/b][/size]
This began as an experiment in adding co-op to a 2003 game through memory scanning and engine hooks, without its source code. The design takes inspiration from Elden Ring's Seamless Co-op: one player hosts, friends drop in, and each keeps their own character. The research notes are public for other modders to use. Other projects use dedicated servers or engine reimplementations; [url=https://github.com/ChristopherVR/kotor-coop#other-kotor-co-op-projects]see the README for alternatives[/url].

[size=5][b]Permissions and credits[/b][/size]
Original mod code is licensed GPL-3.0-only with an additional KOTOR 1 linking permission. You may fork, modify and redistribute under those terms. Third-party notices ship inside the package. Gameplay images depict the original game and retain their underlying rights; the silent GIFs contain no music.

This unofficial fan project is not endorsed by or affiliated with the game's rights holders. No game files or DRM bypass are supplied; each player needs a lawfully acquired game. No publisher permission is claimed. [url=https://github.com/ChristopherVR/kotor-coop/blob/main/docs/LEGAL.md]Licensing and legal notes.[/url]
